import pf.Html
import ../../Demos/Birds
import ../../Demos/Snake
import ../../Demos/Timers
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Callout
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/Feature
import ../../Ui/FeatureGrid
import ../../Ui/Note
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/Demo

meta = {
	title: "Showcase",
	description: "A game of Snake and a flock of bouncing Roc birds: components that Roc, compiled to WebAssembly, runs frame by frame in your browser.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "snake", label: "Snake" },
		{ id: "flock", label: "A flock of Roc birds" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How they work" },
		{ id: "sound", label: "Sound, from a handler" },
		{ id: "timers", label: "Timers" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="Showcase"
				title="A game and a flock, run by Roc"
			>
				They are components like the rest: the server rendered them, and your browser hydrated them. But
				these run on time, not only on clicks. Every animation frame goes to Roc, which moves things;
				only the holes that read what moved run again, and patch the few attributes that changed.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="snake">Snake</Heading>
			<p>
				Click the board, then steer with the arrow keys or WASD; Space pauses, M mutes. The arrows don't
				scroll the page while you play, and Tab still takes you out of the game. Pick a level: Easy, Medium
				and Hard each keep one speed, and on Impossible every fruit makes the snake faster.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo game meter="snake" idle="Roc is idle until you start: no frames run while the game waits.">
			<div data-demo="snake"><Snake seed={7} /></div>
		</Demo>

		<NoHydration>
			<Heading id="flock">A flock of Roc birds</Heading>
			<p>
				Say how many birds, by typing a number or with −5, −1, +1 and +5: new ones come in, or the newest
				fly away. Each one flies in its own direction,
				bounces off the walls and off the others, and squeaks when it bumps into one (once you've
				clicked something: browsers keep pages quiet until then).
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo game meter="birds" idle="Measuring…">
			<div data-demo="birds"><Birds count={4} /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<Note>
				The line under each is measured as it runs, here: each frame is a handler that moves every bird and
				settles every pair that meets, then the holes that read the flock, each bird's place among them,
				and the patches they make.
			</Note>

			<Heading id="how">How they work</Heading>
			<FeatureGrid>
				<Feature title="Time is a handler">
					<p>
						<code>on:frame.ms={"{|ms| send!(Frame(ms))}"}</code> runs its handler every animation frame,
						given the time since the last, while its element is on the page. The snake keeps it on an
						element inside a <code>{"<Show>"}</code> that holds only while you play, so a paused game
						costs nothing; an empty sky has none either.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Keys that do one thing">
					<p>
						<code>on:keydown.ArrowUp={"{|| send!(Turn(Up))}"}</code> runs for that key only, and keeps it
						from scrolling the page. Keys nobody named, Tab among them, do what they always do.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Rows that stay, so little changes">
					<p>
						Birds are <code>{"<For>"}</code> rows keyed by id: a bird keeps its element, and its move is
						two custom properties, which the stylesheet turns into a translation. The snake's segments are
						rows by position (<code>{"keyed={Bool.False}"}</code>): when it moves, each takes the cell of
						the one ahead, and a transition as long as a step slides it there.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Pure, so it replays">
					<p>
						The rules are a plain <code>update</code>, which the component keeps the game's signal with.
						Roc has no hidden randomness: the food's next place and a new bird's heading come from a seed
						in the game (<code>Demos/Random.roc</code>). The same moves give the same game, and every rule is
						an <code>expect</code> away from a test.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="What isn't shown isn't read">
					<p>
						The snake moves one cell every step's worth of frames. The time since its last move is a signal
						of its own, which nothing on the page reads: a frame that only adds to it runs no hole, and
						patches nothing.
					</p>
				</Feature>
			</FeatureGrid>
			<details>
				<summary>The snake: <code>{Sources.demo_snake.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_snake.file} source={Sources.demo_snake.rocx} />
			</details>
			<details>
				<summary>The flock: <code>{Sources.demo_birds.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_birds.file} source={Sources.demo_birds.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="sound">Sound, from a handler</Heading>
			<p>
				The game's rules are pure: <code>update</code> can't make a sound, only the next game. So the next
				game says what its step sounded like (<code>"eat"</code>, <code>"die"</code>, or nothing), which a
				test can check, and the handler that keeps the game's signal plays it, with
				<code>Audio.play!</code>:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.demo_snake.file} source={"go! = |next| {\n\tset_game!(next)\n\tif (!next.muted and !next.sound.is_empty()) Audio.play!(\"/sounds/\${next.sound}.wav\") else {}\n}"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				It plays any file the browser can, WAV, MP3 or Ogg, from the site or anywhere else, with nothing
				prepared in advance: the browser fetches a file the first time, and keeps it. These are a few KB
				each, chiptune notes made by a script (<code>site/sounds.mjs</code>). The flock plays one pop at
				every pitch, with <code>{"Audio.play_with!(url, { volume, rate })"}</code>: a bird more is a higher note,
				the same file a little faster.
			</p>
			<p>
				A sound plays once the handler is done and the page is patched with what it wrote, and a handler
				that waits for the server and runs again plays it once. Only a handler plays: while computing, and on
				the server, it fails. For what the platform has no module for (a vibration, the clipboard), a handler
				calls the page's own JavaScript with <code>{"Browser.call!(name, arg)"}</code>, as Elm's ports do;
				an effect that answers back is a request (see <a href={Paths.calls}>talking to the server</a>).
			</p>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Watch it happen">
				<p>
					Open your browser's element inspector on a bird while it flies: its <code>style</code> is the
					only thing that changes, frame after frame, and only in the hundredths Roc rounds it to.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="timers">Timers</Heading>
			<p>
				A frame is the clock for motion. For “every tenth of a second” and “once the typing stops”, a
				component has the browser's timers, from <code>pf.Solid</code>: <code>set_interval!(ms, tick!)</code>
				and <code>set_timeout!(ms, done!)</code> run a handler later, as a click runs one, and answer its
				<code>clear!</code>, which stops it.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo game>
			<div data-demo="timers"><Timers /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<p>
				The stopwatch keeps its interval's <code>clear!</code> in a signal, as JavaScript keeps an interval's id,
				and Stop calls it:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.demo_timers.file} source={"(watch!, set_watch!) = create_signal!(stopped)\ntoggle! = ||\n\tmatch watch!() {\n\t\tRunning(clear!) => {\n\t\t\tclear!()\n\t\t\tset_watch!(Stopped)\n\t\t}\n\t\tStopped => set_watch!(Running(set_interval!(100, || set_tenths!(tenths!() + 1))))\n\t}"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The search clears the last key's timeout at each key, and starts its own, so it runs once, 400 ms
				after the last. And the clock clears nothing. In Solid, a component stops its own timer with
				<code>{"onCleanup(() => clearInterval(id))"}</code>; here that is built in. A timer belongs to what
				started it: a component's, to the component; a handler's, to the part its element is in. When that
				goes, as the clock does when the <code>{"<Show>"}</code> around it hides it, its timers stop, and the
				ticks heard stop with them.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="note" title="The server starts none">
				<p>
					While the server renders the page, no timer runs: the clock is rendered at 0, and the browser,
					hydrating the page, starts its interval.
				</p>
			</Callout>
			<details>
				<summary>The timers, and the clock: <code>{Sources.demo_timers.file}</code>, <code>{Sources.demo_clock.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_timers.file} source={Sources.demo_timers.rocx} />
				<Code title={Sources.demo_clock.file} source={Sources.demo_clock.rocx} />
			</details>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
